From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 10:47:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tools/libcompat: add the "overlay" profile and the two-sided
 libc shim

`libcompat` so far answers one shape of the "gate source, foreign libc"
problem: stage a hand-picked subset of illumos headers *in place of* the
host's, for the small onbld ELF tools.  That is `include/` plus `common/`.

There is a second shape it does not answer, and it is the opposite one.  A
program that wants illumos' *real* headers -- a filesystem's on-disk format,
say -- puts the whole gate header tree on its include path and needs the host
only for the implementation underneath.  The staged subset is nowhere near
enough for it, and layering the staged profile underneath actively breaks it:
the gate's own `<sys/types.h>` is then in play and `native_compat.h`'s
typedefs collide with it head-on.

What that program needs instead is small and specific.  Three things do not
survive the meeting:

  * `<iso/stdio_iso.h>`  illumos spells the standard streams as slots in a
    libc-owned `__iob[]` array; a foreign libc owns its own `FILE` objects and
    exports pointers, so `__iob` is simply absent at link time.

  * `<sys/stat.h>`, `<sys/statvfs.h>`  `struct stat` is an ABI and not the
    same one: the gate's is 128 bytes where glibc's is 144.  Letting the
    gate's declaration meet the host's implementation does not merely give
    wrong field values, it overruns the caller's buffer by 16 bytes and
    smashes the return address of whoever declared the struct on its stack.

  * `<dirent.h>`  glibc's `struct dirent` carries a `d_type` byte illumos'
    does not, so `d_name` sits one byte further along and every name a
    gate-built caller reads back is shifted by one.

So `overlay/` is a third profile, kept apart from the other two for the same
reason those two are kept apart from each other: a consumer picks exactly one,
by which directory it `-I`s first.  Each file here `#include_next`s the gate's
own header, leaving it to declare everything as usual, and then repoints just
the one declaration that cannot stand -- the three stream names, or the
*calls* whose struct layouts differ.  The gate's `struct stat` stays the one
every caller sees, which is what keeps `S_ISREG()` and friends working.

The redirections are function-like macros on purpose.  An object-like
`#define stat ...` would rewrite every mention of `struct stat` too, including
the ones in these very declarations.

`common/compat_host.c` and `common/compat_gate.c` are the functions behind
those macros, plus the entry points a foreign libc has no counterpart for at
all: `___errno()`, `_sysconf()`.  They are two halves of one shim and they are
split because they must be compiled against *different* headers -- which is
also why `common/compat_priv.h`, the interface between them, may name no libc
type at all and is plain fixed-width integers throughout.

Both halves install as source rather than into `$(LIBRARY)`, since each has to
be compiled with the consumer's own `-I` flags and feature-test macros and
there is no one such view to pick here.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
